 },
 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "What the infinitive is",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "The <b>infinitive</b> is the \"to\" form of a verb: \"to carry\", \"to see\". English uses two words for it. Latin uses one, and it nearly always ends in <em>-re</em>."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "dominus servum <u class=\"c\">laborare</u> <u class=\"v\">iubet</u>.",
      "en": "The master orders the slave <b>to work</b>.",
      "note": "<em>laborare</em> is the infinitive: \"to work\". <em>iubet</em> is \"orders\"."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "The infinitive does not change for the person: <em>portare</em> is always \"to carry\".",
      "It is the second form given in the word list: <em>porto, portare</em>.",
      "<em>sum</em> is irregular: its infinitive is <em>esse</em>, \"to be\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "forms",
   "h2": "The four infinitives",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Conjugation",
       "I ...",
       "Infinitive",
       "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "1st",
        "<em>porto</em>",
        "port<b>are</b>",
        "to carry"
       ],
       [
        "2nd",
        "<em>moneo</em>",
        "mon<b>ere</b>",
        "to warn"
       ],
       [
        "3rd",
        "<em>traho</em>",
        "trah<b>ere</b>",
        "to drag"
       ],
       [
        "4th",
        "<em>audio</em>",
        "aud<b>ire</b>",
        "to hear"
       ],
       [
        "irregular",
        "<em>sum</em>",
        "<b>esse</b>",
        "to be"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "note": "The 2nd and 3rd conjugation infinitives look alike. In <em>monere</em> the first <em>e</em> is long; in <em>trahere</em> it is short. When you translate, it makes no difference: both are \"to\" + the verb."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "iubeo",
   "h2": "Orders with <em>iubeo</em>",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "<em>iubeo</em> means \"I order\". The person given the order goes in the accusative, and what they are to do goes in the infinitive. English works the same way: \"he orders the slave to work\"."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "dominus servos aquam <u class=\"c\">portare</u> <u class=\"v\">iubet</u>.",
      "en": "The master orders the slaves <b>to carry</b> water.",
      "note": "Two accusatives: <em>servos</em> are the people ordered, and <em>aquam</em> is what they carry."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "nuntius pueros <u class=\"c\">venire</u> <u class=\"v\">iubet</u>.",
      "en": "The messenger orders the boys <b>to come</b>."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Translating an order",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Find <em>iubet</em> or <em>iubent</em> and the subject: who gives the order?",
      "Then the accusative: who is ordered? Put them straight after \"orders\".",
      "Then the infinitive with \"to\", and anything that goes with it: \"to carry water\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "<em>portare</em> is \"to carry\". An infinitive has no person: \"he carries\" is <em>portat</em>.",
      "After <em>iubeo</em>, the accusative is the person ordered: <em>servum laborare iubet</em> is \"orders the slave to work\".",
      "<em>monere</em> and <em>trahere</em> look alike, but the English is still just \"to warn\", \"to drag\".",
      "\"Tell\" is a good translation of <em>iubeo</em> in an order: \"he tells the slave to work\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
